A||N||4||MrFeeArrow||134a Conversion results||||1||
Z||000000||MrFeeArrow||06-01-2001||10:28 PM||||Now for the results of 134a conversion:<BR>Test drive #1<BR>Ambient temp. 90 degrees and clear,....very cold in max, can notice the clutch cycle every 15 seconds or so. Selecting HI the clutch does not cylce at all and the air is still very cool<BR>Test drive #2<BR>Ambient temp. 73 degrees with thunderstorms....still verycold in max with clutch cycling as before. Selecting HI is still very cold with same clutch cycles, selecting low and max has same results.<P>Note: The clutch stays engaged and never cycles while at idle.<P>Overall this conversion seems to be a success, the only question I have is the cycling of the clutch. It would seem that the system is low on freon and that the low pressure switch is doing it's job? Or that the system under cooler outside ambient temps compensates this way?<P>What do you say experts? <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||voyagerspe||06-01-2001||11:01 PM||||I am by no means an expert however you can adjust the switch with a small screwdriver to get the compressor to cycle at idle.  <BR>I put 3 cans in my system in all 3 of my fieros they cool great.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrFeeArrow||06-01-2001||11:08 PM||||OK,...here's the break down from the reciept for all parts and labor.<BR>1.Compressor...............394.00<BR>2.Accumulator..............125.93<BR>3.Orifice.................. 8.38<BR>4.Flush.................... 60.00<BR>5.Evac&Recharge............ 84.00<BR>6.Freon 2.5 lbs............ 75.00<BR>7.Labor....................210.00<BR>8.TAX MAN.................. 67.85<BR>Total...................1033.68<P>This seems to be the going rate for our area.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||thomas_l||06-02-2001||07:00 AM||||Some cycling with low a/c load is normal. If the outside temp is relatively cool and the blower is on low, the clutch will cycle because the evaporator pressure is low. It would be a good idea to get it checked anyway while it's still under warranty.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Rodrv6||06-02-2001||08:34 AM||||It is normal for the clutch not to cycle when the engine is idling and the demand on the a/c is high.  I wouldn't adjust the pressure switch just to make the compressor cycle at idle.  To do it correctly, you would need to hook up a set of gauges and adjust the switch to get the desired low side cycling pressure.  If it's cooling now, I wouldn't mess with it.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
